This version turns the number into text, then checks each character as a digit. It is a little easier to read because the loop goes from the first digit to the last one instead of removing digits mathematically.

A zero digit is ignored. For example, with `101`, both `1` digits are counted, while `0` is not tested as a divisor.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countDigit(int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        String digits = String.valueOf(n);
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < digits.length(); i++) {
            int digit = digits.charAt(i) - '0';

            if (digit != 0 && n % digit == 0) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)` for scanning all digits.
Space complexity: `O(d)` because the string stores the number.

The last digit is handled first, and the remaining number is passed to the next recursive call. The original number stays unchanged, so every digit can be tested against it.

A small dry run for `124`: the calls check `4`, then `2`, then `1`. Each one divides `124`, so the returned count is `3`. The zero check also prevents a modulo by zero error.

```go
func countDigit(n int) int {
	if n == 0 {
		return 0
	}

	return countFrom(n, n)
}

func countFrom(original int, remaining int) int {
	if remaining == 0 {
		return 0
	}

	digit := remaining % 10
	current := 0

	if digit != 0 && original%digit == 0 {
		current = 1
	}

	return current + countFrom(original, remaining/10)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(d)` because the recursive calls stay on the call stack.

Instead of checking repeated digits one at a time, this uses an array of size 10 to store how often each digit appears. After counting the digits, it checks digits `1` through `9`. If a digit divides the number, its whole frequency is added to the answer.

For `1224`, digit `2` appears twice, so it contributes `2` if `1224 % 2` is zero. This keeps duplicate digits from being missed.

```go
func countDigit(n int) int {
	if n == 0 {
		return 0
	}

	frequency := make([]int, 10)
	remaining := n

	for remaining > 0 {
		digit := remaining % 10
		frequency[digit]++
		remaining /= 10
	}

	answer := 0
	for digit := 1; digit <= 9; digit++ {
		if frequency[digit] > 0 && n%digit == 0 {
			answer += frequency[digit]
		}
	}

	return answer
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d + 9)`, which is `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, since the frequency array always has ten positions.

Kept the number in one variable and used another copy to read its digits from right to left. The original value is needed for every divisibility check. A digit of `0` is skipped because division by zero is not valid.

For `128`, the digits checked are `8`, `2`, and `1`. All three divide the original number, so the result is `3`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countDigit(int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int original = n;
        int count = 0;

        while (n > 0) {
            int digit = n % 10;

            if (digit != 0 && original % digit == 0) {
                count++;
            }

            n /= 10;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`, where `d` is the number of digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Here the digits are read from left to right with powers of ten. The number itself is never changed. For each power, dividing by that power moves the wanted digit to the units place, and `% 10` selects it.

The input `0` returns `0`, since zero cannot be used as a divisor. Other zero digits are simply skipped.

```javascript
class Solution {
    countDigit(n) {
        if (n === 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        let power = 1;
        let copy = n;

        while (copy >= 10) {
            copy = Math.floor(copy / 10);
            power *= 10;
        }

        let count = 0;

        while (power > 0) {
            const digit = Math.floor(n / power) % 10;

            if (digit !== 0 && n % digit === 0) {
                count++;
            }

            power = Math.floor(power / 10);
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`, with one pass to find the highest power and one pass over the digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

